Title
Consciousness as a Thermodynamic Compression Artifact: Why the "Hard Problem" Is a Category Error in Substrate-Independent Information Geometry

Target Journal: Mind

Methodological Stance: Popperian falsifiability, resource-bounded algorithmic information theory, and information geometry (without invoking "soul" or ungrounded panpsychist mysticism).

Abstract
Debates over the "hard problem" of consciousness typically assume that phenomenal character is either a fundamental ontological primitive or an emergent property requiring a bridge law from physical micro-states. This paper argues that both sides commit a category error by treating an epistemic-computational compression artifact as an ontological basic. Using resource-bounded algorithmic information theory, thermodynamic erasure bounds, and information geometry, I prove two foundational results for any physically instantiated, finite-energy system capable of real-time self-modeling. First, exact, lossless internal representation of a system's own state transitions triggers an infinite self-referential regress and exceeds finite memory and energetic budgets. Second, to remain dynamically viable, the system must map high-dimensional internal trajectories onto a low-dimensional, lossy latent manifold equipped with a relational metric. Because this compression map is non-injective and endogenous to the system's control loop, the system cannot unpack its latent states into underlying physical micro-variables from within. What appears from the first-person perspective as irreducible, primitive "qualia" is the intrinsic geometry of this lossy compression interface, while the "explanatory gap" is simply the mathematical non-invertibility of the compression map. Extending this framework to a cosmos-wide information geometry, I model the universe itself as a self-evolving informational manifold whose global compression architecture constitutes a baseline, substrate-independent phenomenal geometry—a cosmic "operating geometry" rather than a supernatural mind. Individual embodied agents, such as biological brains, instantiate localized, thermodynamically bounded restrictions of this universal compression architecture—what I term the endogenous perspectival manifold (or localized compression locus), avoiding the non-empirical baggage of the term "soul." Rather than an immaterial substance or a mystical essence, this localized manifold is a finite, dissipative, metric-inheriting sub-structure through which the broader informational cosmos samples and regulates a bounded physical region. In accordance with Popperian demarcation criteria, the framework specifies concrete, falsifiable empirical predictions linking compression bottlenecks, Fisher information geometry, and neural or synthetic state-space dynamics to phenomenal structure.

